Z||000000||EdsB52||02-16-2001||06:28 PM||||I found a 3.8 supercharged engine in a 98 Bonneville at a junkyard. The counter attendant told me it was complete w/8,000 miles on it and he wanted $1800 for the engine, computer, all accessories, and wire harnesses. I went to check it out and found that the engine wire harness was missing, as well as the A/C compressor. The speedometer/odometer was also gone so I can't verify the mileage, but the engine ressembled a low mile engine. That supercharger looks shweet and gave me wood! My question is; how difficult is it to procure or manufacture the engine wire harness? Any help would be appreciated, even if you want to sell me one!||||reg||1
Z||000001||Formula88||02-16-2001||07:29 PM||||I'd be wary since you've already found out the engine is not what he represented.  Typically wiring harnesses are expensive and a PITA!  I'd keep looking for a complete setup.  Maybe then you could get the missing parts off of it if it's engine doesn't look as good.  <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero_Inside_1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||EdsB52||02-16-2001||10:27 PM||||I agree. The yard where the car was located was a mile from the main yard, where another attendant was waiting to show me the car. So I got home and called the initial clerk who quoted me the price and told him of the condition. He replied by being surprised and then all of the sudden remembering he had just got another car in, a 96 Grand Prix w/a 3.8 supercharged. I think they know they F*cked-up by either selling the engine harness from the engine, or that the car had more mileage, or something jaded like that. I found another place in town that has a 96 with supposedly 15,000 miles that wants 1,100 for theirs, but no engine harness. WTF, are these guys dumb asses? Since I probably won't have the engine installed and running for 90 days, I'm basically buying the engine as is. Either way, I'm going to do the upgrade 'cause I got the cash and I'm jonesing for it! I can't wait to blow away Camaros, eclipse's, pudstains (mustangs), and some vettes who walked all over my tired-ass 2.8. Late.......||||reg||1
Z||000003||Shaun41178||02-16-2001||10:36 PM||||it has been found by me that when those j-yard worker people pull engines from the car they cutup the wire harness to get the engine out instead of disconnecting each clip.  if the engine is still in the car then I guess they are Dumb@sses somehow for not having the harness<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||DRH||02-16-2001||11:31 PM||||Yep... I've watched junk yard guys remove an engine armed with nothing but a tow truck, wire cutters and a torch.<P>Unless their in the business to sell for conversions, or you talk to them BEFORE they pull it, they don't usually worry too much about anything but the engine itself. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Slammed Fiero||02-17-2001||12:19 AM||||I see after 5 years my fiero inside graphic is still alive and well....hehe<BR><P>------------------<BR>Jonathan M McCreery<BR>86 GT<BR>New Custom White face gauges 17" Tr motorsports Typhoons Ground Control coil overs , Big bars front and rear , Mustang scoop , accel coil , no cat , 4spd , short shift , poly dog bone and bushings momo pedals and shift knob , round headlight conversion , Geni stainless steel oval exhaust tips. Pioneer 6000 CD player , Sony Mobile Amp. Currently doing big brake conversion...<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/jssig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||gabriel||02-17-2001||12:35 AM||||Hu Eds:<P>I'm about to swap for the 3800SC,as well.<P>  No one better than Terryk to go to for advice on this swap, and I quote from him on an earlier post:<P>&gt;"$1850 is a good price if the miles are low. Make sure it includes the PCM, wiring harness, about 2ft of the fuel lines, the pipe from the rear manifold to the cat, the air filter box and purge canister." <P>Note that the price of $1850 included everything AND the 4T65E AOD trans, with delivery to my door, as well. (from a 97 Olds LSS) <P>"Complete" seems to be a different word in salvage business.<P>Personally, I would pass on this engine.<BR>You will have nothing but expensive problems locating a wiring harness to match the car/engine/options/trans  that the engine/PCM was orginally configured for.<P> Keep searching for the complete package.<BR> Good luck!<P>Just my .02.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||terryk||02-17-2001||12:39 AM||||See if they will give you a harness from a non-SC engine of the same year or close. Most of the wires will be the same (injector, sensors, DIS, etc). The boost control solenoid is likely the odd man out. You're going to know the wiring better than the inside of your eyelids before you are done anyway.||||reg||1
Z||000008||87GTZ34||02-17-2001||01:12 AM||||See if you can get me a VIN.  I have a current carfax account and I'm in the market for such engines  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">.  If the car has a history all the way to the bone yard consider it.  If not, I would not be surprised if they are lying thru their teeth to make and extra $500. How do I know this you ask,  well because it happened to me.  Now I trust no one when it comes to horse trade'n iron...||||reg||1
Z||000009||Formula88||02-17-2001||11:47 AM||||Yea, Slammed, that one's too good NOT to use!||||reg||10
Z||000010||EdsB52||02-17-2001||09:36 PM||||Thanks for all the excellent info and advice guys. I will get the VIN when I go back on Monday to check out the 96 he was referring to and immediately post it. I realize that you can't tell everything, or for that matter anything from the out side of the engine, but I find it plausible that this car has 8,000 miles on it. Also, it was involved in a hard right-rear smacker and not a roll-over. Other than that, there aren't any 3.8 SC motors in the Phoenix area. I will travel out of state if I have to, but I want to hear it run. I would love to buy a whole smashed car, remove what I want, part out what is good, and sh!t-can the rest. But I'm probably asking too much for that to happen. Keep posting good stuff. Out.||||reg||1
Z||000011||gabriel||02-18-2001||07:04 AM||||  Eds, why limit yourself to local?  Lots of people think that shipping is too expensive, but all the big yards have Roadway accounts.<P>  In my case, the delivery added ~$200, from NY to Fl.  I have found a lot of 3800SCs, abiet with a huge price range.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Songman||02-18-2001||10:53 AM||||Yeah, I'm about to do the 3800s/c swap too. We have a guy in our TN group that has brand new pull out engines! I think the price for them is about $1800 too, but that is just for the engine. Check out our site and give him a call if you want details... He knows his stuff! Link is in sig. Look for the Grooms logo for his name and number.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif">  <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A><p>[This message has been edited by Songman (edited 02-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||lowCG||02-20-2001||08:06 PM||||Terry is right,the sensors are all the same,so you could get a harness from an Oldsmobile and just use the plugs from it.You need to ohm out each wire from the sensors back to the ECM plug,and re-route the whole mess so it better fits into your car anyways.I'd make sure the ECM was there for sure,and also be sure and get the aircleaner/airflow meter,unless you're going to use a '95 or earlier ECM like Terry does.<BR>You may be able to use the fact that the harness is missing to strike a bargain.||||reg||1
